/// The `wa-email` component's own world: the kernel service contract, the bus fact it publishes, the
/// queue it works, and the one interface this connector needs that the kernel does not serve yet: a
/// host-side TLS byte stream.
///
/// `include` pulls in `wa:kernel/service-component@0.2.0` unchanged (websocket/http/speech/kv/
/// lifecycle/log/credentials in, `service.run` out). IMAP and SMTP are line protocols over a TLS
/// session, so they cannot ride `websocket` (frames) or `http` (one exchange). `wa:email/transport`
/// below is that seam: **the host dials, terminates TLS and moves bytes; the guest speaks IMAP and
/// SMTP inside the stream**, exactly as `wa-whatsapp` speaks the WhatsApp protocol inside a kernel
/// websocket frame. Serving it is the one thing `wa-host` has to add for this component to start
/// (`REPORT.md`, "What integration into wa-host needs"); the grant is the manifest's `net` list, so a
/// connector can only reach the host:port its definition names.
///
/// `deps/kernel.wit`, `deps/clocks.wit`, `deps/queue/queue.wit` and `deps/bus/bus.wit` are snapshots of
/// the published contracts (`wit/kernel-v2/`, `wit/queue/`, `wit/bus/`), the same arrangement
/// `wa-whatsapp` and `svc-queue-echo` use; `scripts/test-email-connector.lua` compares them byte for
/// byte and fails when they drift.
package wa:email@0.1.0;

/// A TLS byte stream to one granted `host:port`. The guest never sees the TLS session, the
/// certificate chain or the key: the kernel owns them, like it owns the whatsapp websocket's.
interface transport {
    use wa:kernel/types@0.2.0.{error};

    resource connection {
        /// Open a TLS stream (or plaintext, only for a loopback test host) to `host:port` if the
        /// component's `net` grant names it. Suspends while the TCP+TLS handshake runs; a host outside
        /// the grant is `refused`, and that refusal is the only answer that names the host.
        connect: static async func(host: string, port: u16, tls: bool) -> result<connection, error>;
        /// Up to `max` bytes as soon as the peer sent some. An **empty list is end of stream** (the
        /// peer closed), never "nothing yet": a guest that cannot tell those apart would spin.
        read: async func(max: u32) -> result<list<u8>, error>;
        /// Write every byte; suspends while the kernel's outbound buffer is full.
        write: async func(data: list<u8>) -> result<_, error>;
        /// Close; further reads answer `closed`.
        close: func();
    }
}

/// A long-lived email connector: kernel interfaces in, `service` out.
world email-service {
    include wa:kernel/service-component@0.2.0;
    /// The kernel's service world imports the monotonic clock (for waiting). A queue job's freshness
    /// window and a message's arrival time are wall time, so the wall clock is imported too: standard
    /// WASI, no grant of its own.
    import wasi:clocks/system-clock@0.3.0;
    import transport;
    import wa:bus/publisher@0.1.0;
    import wa:queue/worker@0.1.0;
}
